# Describe cost management in Azure + Describe features and tools for
# governance and compliance. Per test 9, drill-only 7.
ITEMS = [
    # ---- Test 1 ----
    S(1, 1, "Pricing calculator",
      "Before deploying anything, a company wants an estimate of the monthly cost of two virtual machines and a storage account. Which tool should it use?",
      "Azure pricing calculator",
      ["Microsoft Cost Management", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Service Health"],
      "The pricing calculator estimates the cost of Azure services you plan to use, before you deploy them. Microsoft Cost Management reports on and forecasts what you are actually spending on resources that already exist."),
    S(1, 1, "Budgets",
      "A team wants an email when its subscription's spending reaches 80 percent of a monthly limit. What should it set up?",
      "A budget with an alert in Cost Management",
      ["A ReadOnly lock on the subscription", "An Azure Policy rule with the Deny effect", "A Service Health alert for the company's region"],
      "Cost Management budgets set a spending threshold for a scope and send alerts when spending reaches the percentages you choose. A lock or a Deny policy controls changes to resources, not spending, and Service Health reports Azure incidents."),
    S(1, 2, "Tags",
      "A finance team wants to report Azure costs by department across many resource groups and subscriptions. What should be applied to resources?",
      "Tags such as Department: Finance",
      ["Resource locks on every resource", "Availability sets for each department", "A separate region for each department"],
      "Tags are name and value pairs added to resources, and Cost Management can group and filter costs by tag, across resource groups and subscriptions. Locks and availability sets do nothing for cost reporting."),
    YN(1, 2, "Factors that affect cost",
       "For each statement about Azure costs, select Yes if it is true or No if it is false.",
       [("The same resource can cost a different amount in different Azure regions.", True),
        ("Inbound data transfer into Azure datacenters is generally free.", True),
        ("Outbound data transfer is free between every pair of Azure regions.", False),
        ("Deallocating a VM stops compute charges, but its disks are still billed.", True)],
       "Prices vary by region, and bandwidth is billed mainly on outbound traffic: inbound data is generally free, while data leaving a region or Azure is charged, including traffic between regions. A deallocated VM stops compute charges, but its managed disks keep costing money."),
    S(1, 1, "Azure Policy",
      "A company must make sure resources can be created only in approved Azure regions. What should it use?",
      "Azure Policy",
      ["Resource locks", "Microsoft Purview", "Azure Advisor"],
      "Azure Policy enforces rules on resource properties, such as allowed locations or VM sizes, and can deny non-compliant deployments. Resource locks stop deletion or changes to existing resources but cannot limit where new ones are created."),
    S(1, 2, "Resource locks",
      "An administrator wants to stop anyone, including subscription Owners, from deleting a production database by mistake, while still allowing changes to it. What should be applied?",
      "A Delete (CanNotDelete) lock",
      ["A ReadOnly lock", "The Reader role for all users", "A Cost Management budget"],
      "A Delete lock means authorized users can read and modify a resource but cannot delete it, and locks apply to every user and role, Owners included. A ReadOnly lock would also block the changes the team still needs to make."),
    S(1, 1, "Microsoft Purview",
      "Which service helps a company discover, classify and govern its data across Azure, on-premises and multicloud sources?",
      "Microsoft Purview",
      ["Azure Policy", "Microsoft Defender for Cloud", "Azure Monitor"],
      "Microsoft Purview is a family of data governance, risk and compliance solutions that gives one view of data wherever it lives: it maps data, classifies sensitive information and tracks lineage. Azure Policy governs Azure resource settings, not the data inside them."),
    M(1, 2, "Reducing costs",
      "Which two actions reduce Azure costs? (Choose two.)",
      ["Deallocate VMs that are not being used", "Use Azure Hybrid Benefit with existing licenses"],
      ["Apply tags to every resource", "Add a ReadOnly lock to each VM", "Switch all storage accounts to geo-redundant storage"],
      "Deallocating idle VMs stops their compute charges, and Azure Hybrid Benefit lets you bring eligible Windows Server and SQL Server licenses to pay a lower rate. Tags help you see costs but do not reduce them, and geo-redundant storage costs more than local redundancy."),
    MT(1, 2, "Governance tools",
       "Match each requirement to the tool that meets it.",
       ["Azure Policy", "Resource locks", "Microsoft Purview", "Tags"],
       [("Block creation of VMs larger than an approved size", 0),
        ("Prevent accidental deletion of a resource", 1),
        ("Map and classify sensitive data across many sources", 2),
        ("Label resources with their cost center", 3)],
       "Azure Policy enforces rules on resource properties such as VM size. Locks protect existing resources from deletion or change. Purview governs data, and tags add metadata such as cost center that can be used for reporting."),

    # ---- Test 2 ----
    S(2, 1, "Cost Management",
      "Which tool shows how much each resource group spent last month and forecasts future spending?",
      "Microsoft Cost Management",
      ["The Azure pricing calculator", "Azure Service Health", "Microsoft Purview"],
      "Cost analysis in Microsoft Cost Management breaks down actual spending by scope, resource group, service or tag, and forecasts where spending is heading. The pricing calculator only estimates services you have not deployed yet."),
    S(2, 2, "Policy initiatives",
      "What is an Azure Policy initiative?",
      "A group of related policy definitions assigned together",
      ["A lock that blocks changes to a group of resources", "A spending limit applied to a management group", "A report of Advisor recommendations to improve reliability"],
      "An initiative, also called a policy set, groups related policy definitions so you can assign and track them as one, for example everything needed for a compliance standard. Locks, budgets and Advisor reports are separate tools."),
    S(2, 1, "ReadOnly locks",
      "What does a ReadOnly lock on a resource do?",
      "Users can read it but cannot change or delete it",
      ["Only Owners can change it, and no one can delete it", "Users can change it but cannot delete it", "It hides the resource from everyone except Owners"],
      "A ReadOnly lock lets authorized users read a resource but not update or delete it, whatever their role. Allowing changes while blocking deletion describes a Delete (CanNotDelete) lock."),
    M(2, 2, "Factors that affect cost",
      "Which two factors affect the cost of Azure resources? (Choose two.)",
      ["The region the resource is deployed in", "The amount of outbound network traffic"],
      ["The number of tags on the resource", "The name of the resource group", "The number of management groups above it"],
      "Cost depends on factors such as resource type, how much you consume, the region, network traffic (outbound data transfer), the subscription type and Marketplace purchases. Tags, names and the management group structure are free organizing tools."),
    S(2, 3, "Policy effects",
      "A company wants to find existing resources that are missing a required tag, without blocking any deployments yet. What should it do?",
      "Assign an Azure Policy with the Audit effect",
      ["Assign an Azure Policy with the Deny effect", "Apply a Delete lock to each of the resource groups", "Create a budget for each resource group"],
      "The Audit effect marks non-compliant resources in the compliance report without stopping anything, which suits a first look at the problem. Deny would block new non-compliant deployments, which the company does not want yet."),
    YN(2, 1, "Tags",
       "For each statement about resource tags, select Yes if it is true or No if it is false.",
       [("Tags are name and value pairs.", True),
        ("Resources automatically inherit the tags of their resource group.", False),
        ("Tags can be used to group costs in Cost Management.", True),
        ("A tag prevents a resource from being deleted.", False)],
       "A tag is a name and value pair used to organize resources and report costs. Resources do not inherit tags from their resource group by default, though Azure Policy can add or inherit them. Preventing deletion is the job of a resource lock."),
    S(2, 2, "Lock inheritance",
      "An administrator applies a Delete lock to a resource group. What is the effect?",
      "Resources in the group inherit the lock",
      ["Only the group itself is protected from deletion", "Owners can still delete the resources inside", "The lock expires automatically after 24 hours"],
      "A lock applied at a parent scope is inherited by every resource in that scope, including resources added later. Locks apply to all users and roles, and they stay in place until someone with permission removes them."),
    S(2, 3, "Policy and RBAC",
      "What is the main difference between Azure Policy and Azure role-based access control (RBAC)?",
      "Policy governs resource properties; RBAC governs user actions",
      ["Policy governs user actions; RBAC governs resource properties",
       "Policy controls spending limits; RBAC controls region choices",
       "Policy and RBAC are two names for the same feature in Azure"],
      "RBAC decides which actions a user can take, at which scope. Azure Policy decides what state resources may be in, whoever creates them. They work together: a Contributor can create resources, but Policy can still deny a VM size or region that is not allowed."),
    S(2, 2, "Azure Hybrid Benefit",
      "A company has Windows Server licenses with Software Assurance. Which offer lets it use those licenses to lower the cost of Azure VMs?",
      "Azure Hybrid Benefit",
      ["Azure Spot Virtual Machines", "An Azure savings plan for compute", "An Azure free account"],
      "Azure Hybrid Benefit lets you apply eligible on-premises Windows Server and SQL Server licenses with Software Assurance to Azure, so you pay a reduced rate. Savings plans and Spot VMs lower costs in other ways and do not use existing licenses."),

    # ---- Drill only ----
    S(0, 1, "Pricing calculator",
      "What does the Azure pricing calculator do?",
      "Estimates costs for services before you deploy them",
      ["Shows the amount you were billed for last month", "Blocks any deployment that would go over a set budget", "Recommends ways to cut your current spending"],
      "The pricing calculator lets you choose services, regions, tiers and options to estimate costs ahead of time. It does not see your real bill; past spending is shown in Cost Management and savings recommendations come from Azure Advisor."),
    S(0, 2, "Budgets",
      "A Cost Management budget threshold is reached. What happens by default?",
      "An alert is sent and resources keep running",
      ["Every resource in the scope is stopped", "New deployments in the scope are blocked", "The subscription is disabled until next month"],
      "Budgets raise alerts when spending crosses the thresholds you set; on their own they do not stop or block anything. You can link a budget to an action group to automate a response, but that is something you set up yourself."),
    S(0, 2, "Microsoft Purview",
      "Which statement describes Microsoft Purview?",
      "A family of data governance, risk and compliance solutions",
      ["A service that enforces rules on Azure resource settings", "A tool that estimates the monthly cost of planned Azure services", "A dashboard of Azure outages that affect you"],
      "Microsoft Purview brings data governance, risk and compliance together: it maps and catalogs data across sources and helps protect sensitive information. Enforcing rules on resource settings is Azure Policy, and outages are shown in Service Health."),
    YN(0, 2, "Azure Policy",
       "For each statement about Azure Policy, select Yes if it is true or No if it is false.",
       [("Azure Policy can stop non-compliant resources from being created.", True),
        ("A policy assigned to a management group applies to the subscriptions under it.", True),
        ("Azure Policy grants users permission to create resources.", False),
        ("Azure Policy evaluates only resources created after the policy was assigned.", False)],
       "Policies can deny non-compliant deployments and are inherited by child scopes. They evaluate existing resources too, and report them as non-compliant, with remediation available for some effects. Permissions to act come from RBAC, not from Policy."),
    S(0, 1, "Removing a lock",
      "An Owner tries to delete a resource that has a Delete lock. What must happen first?",
      "The lock must be removed",
      ["The Owner must sign in again", "The resource must be tagged", "Microsoft support must approve it"],
      "Locks apply to everyone, including Owners. To delete a locked resource, someone with permission to manage locks must first remove the lock; this extra step is what prevents accidental deletion."),
    M(0, 1, "Uses for tags",
      "Which two are common uses of resource tags? (Choose two.)",
      ["Grouping costs by project or department", "Finding resources in one environment"],
      ["Encrypting a resource's data", "Preventing a resource's deletion", "Granting users access to a resource group"],
      "Tags add metadata for resource management, cost reporting, operations and automation, such as marking everything in the test environment or charging costs back to a department. Encryption, locks and RBAC do the other jobs."),
    S(0, 2, "Factors that affect cost",
      "Two virtual machines are the same size and run the same hours, but one costs more each month. What is the most likely reason?",
      "They are deployed in different Azure regions",
      ["They are in different resource groups with different names", "They have different tags", "They are in different management groups"],
      "Azure prices vary by region because of local costs, so the same VM size can cost more in one region than another. Resource groups, tags and management groups organize resources but do not change their price."),
]
